    body: `
<p>English builds a sentence like a train: who, the verb, what, and then the details trail behind: "I'm eating <i>at home</i> <i>tomorrow</i>." Chinese does the opposite. The details come first, and the action waits until the end. Once you know the line-up, you can build long sentences without guessing.</p>
<h3>The line-up</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>When</th><th>Where</th><th>Verb + what</th></tr><tr><td class="k">我</td><td class="k">明天</td><td class="k">在家</td><td class="k">吃饭</td></tr><tr><td class="k">我们</td><td class="k">今天下午</td><td class="k">在图书馆</td><td class="k">学习</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:我明天在家吃饭。|Wǒ míngtiān zài jiā chīfàn.|I'm eating at home tomorrow.}}
{{ex:我们今天下午在图书馆学习。|Wǒmen jīntiān xiàwǔ zài túshūguǎn xuéxí.|We're studying at the library this afternoon.}}
<p>You can leave out the when or the where, but the slots never swap: when comes before where, and both come before the verb.</p>
<h3>Time can also go first</h3>
<p>A time word may move to the very front, before who. <b>明天我去北京</b> <i>Míngtiān wǒ qù Běijīng</i> means the same as <b>我明天去北京</b>; putting the time first just sets the scene. What a time word can never do is go to the end, after the verb, the way it does in English.</p>
{{ex:明天我去北京。|Míngtiān wǒ qù Běijīng.|Tomorrow I'm going to Beijing.}}
{{ex:我明天去北京。|Wǒ míngtiān qù Běijīng.|I'm going to Beijing tomorrow.}}
<h3>How you do it: also before the verb</h3>
<p>"By subway" is a detail too, so it comes before the main verb. <b>坐</b> <i>zuò</i> means to sit, and also to ride: <b>坐地铁去上班</b> is literally "sit subway go to work". You'll see more of this pattern in the lesson on two verbs in a row.</p>
{{ex:我每天坐地铁去上班。|Wǒ měitiān zuò dìtiě qù shàngbān.|I take the subway to work every day.}}
<h3>Big before small</h3>
<p>Chinese moves from the big picture down to the detail. A time goes day, then part of the day, then the hour. A date goes month, then day. A place goes country, then city.</p>
{{ex:我星期六上午十点在公园跑步。|Wǒ xīngqīliù shàngwǔ shí diǎn zài gōngyuán pǎobù.|I go running in the park at ten on Saturday morning.}}
<h3>A few verbs break the rule</h3>
<p><b>住</b> <i>zhù</i> (to live) is the one you have already met: with it, <b>在</b> + place comes after the verb. Say <b>我住在北京</b>, and notice the big-before-small order of the place.</p>
{{ex:我住在中国北京。|Wǒ zhù zài Zhōngguó Běijīng.|I live in Beijing, China.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving the time or place at the end, as in English. <b>我去北京明天</b> → <b>我明天去北京</b>. <b>我吃饭在家</b> → <b>我在家吃饭</b>.</div>
`,
