    title: "了: done, and changed (not a past tense)",
    blurb: "Use 了 to say an action is done or something has changed, and know when to leave it out.",
    deck: "Travel and transport",
    body: `
<p>English marks time on the verb: go, went, gone. Chinese verbs never change. <b>我去北京</b> can be yesterday, today or tomorrow, and a time word tells you which. So <b>了</b> <i>le</i> is not a past tense. It does two other jobs.</p>
<h3>Job 1: the action is done</h3>
<p><b>了</b> right after a verb says the action happened and is complete. It sounds most natural when the object has a number.</p>
{{ex:我买了两张票。|Wǒ mǎi le liǎng zhāng piào.|I bought two tickets.}}
{{ex:他喝了三杯咖啡。|Tā hē le sān bēi kāfēi.|He drank three cups of coffee.}}
<p>When the object has no number, put <b>了</b> at the end of the sentence instead. Verb-object words such as <b>吃饭</b> <i>chīfàn</i> (sometimes called separable verbs) do the same: <b>我吃饭了</b>, "I've eaten".</p>
{{ex:我昨天去上海了。|Wǒ zuótiān qù Shànghǎi le.|I went to Shanghai yesterday.}}
<h3>Job 2: something has changed</h3>
<p><b>了</b> at the end of a sentence can also mean "now it's so, and before it wasn't". English often says "now", "has gotten" or "already".</p>
{{ex:天气冷了。|Tiānqì lěng le.|It's gotten cold.}}
{{ex:我饿了。|Wǒ è le.|I'm hungry now.}}
{{ex:我们到了！|Wǒmen dào le!|We're here!}}
<h3>When there is no 了</h3>
<ul><li><b>Habits in the past.</b> "I used to take the subway a lot" is <b>我以前常常坐地铁</b>, with no <b>了</b>.</li><li><b>States in the past.</b> "I was busy yesterday" is <b>我昨天很忙</b>. The time word already says when.</li><li><b>After 没.</b> <b>没</b> says the action did not happen, so there is nothing to complete: <b>我昨天没去</b>.</li></ul>
{{ex:我以前常常坐地铁。|Wǒ yǐqián chángcháng zuò dìtiě.|I used to take the subway a lot.}}
{{ex:我昨天很忙。|Wǒ zuótiān hěn máng.|I was busy yesterday.}}
{{ex:我昨天没去。|Wǒ zuótiān méi qù.|I didn't go yesterday.}}
<h3>Asking about it</h3>
<p>To ask if something is done, add <b>吗</b> after <b>了</b>. Answer yes with the verb and <b>了</b>, and no with <b>没</b> and the verb, without <b>了</b>.</p>
{{ex:你吃饭了吗？|Nǐ chīfàn le ma?|Have you eaten?}}
<h3>A quick test</h3>
<p>Before you add <b>了</b>, ask: did one action get done, or did something change? If you are only describing how things were, leave it out.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> adding 了 to everything in the past, the way English adds -ed. <b>我昨天很忙了</b> → <b>我昨天很忙</b>. <b>我昨天没去了</b> → <b>我昨天没去</b>.</div>
`,
