    title: "在 and 正在: doing it right now",
    blurb: "Say what someone is doing right now, and ask what they are doing.",
    deck: "Work and study",
    body: `
<p>English shows an action in progress with "am / is / are" and -ing: "He is watching TV." Chinese has no -ing, and it does not use <b>是</b> for "is" here. It puts a small word before the verb instead.</p>
<h3>在 + verb</h3>
<p>You met <b>在</b> <i>zài</i> as "at". Right before a verb it means "in the middle of". This is the everyday spoken form.</p>
{{ex:他在看电视。|Tā zài kàn diànshì.|He's watching TV.}}
{{ex:你在做什么？|Nǐ zài zuò shénme?|What are you doing?}}
<p>The question word stays where the answer goes, as always: <b>你在做什么？</b> "I'm watching TV" answers it with the same shape: <b>我在看电视</b>.</p>
<h3>正在, and 呢 at the end</h3>
<p><b>正在</b> <i>zhèngzài</i> stresses "right at this moment". <b>呢</b> <i>ne</i> at the end of the sentence adds the same "right now" feeling and is very common in speech. You can use one, two or all of them: <b>在…</b>, <b>正在…</b>, <b>在…呢</b>, <b>正在…呢</b>.</p>
{{ex:我正在吃饭呢。|Wǒ zhèngzài chīfàn ne.|I'm eating right now.}}
{{ex:妈妈在打电话呢。|Māma zài dǎ diànhuà ne.|Mom is on the phone.}}
<h3>Saying where</h3>
<p>To say where the action is happening, put <b>在</b> + place before the verb, as you learned. That sentence can already mean "is doing", and <b>呢</b> at the end makes it clear. You do not need a second <b>在</b>.</p>
{{ex:他在家看电视呢。|Tā zài jiā kàn diànshì ne.|He's at home watching TV.}}
<h3>Not for plans</h3>
<p>English also uses -ing for the future: "I'm going to Beijing tomorrow." That is a plan, not an action in progress, so Chinese uses no <b>在</b>: just a time word before the verb.</p>
{{ex:我明天去北京。|Wǒ míngtiān qù Běijīng.|I'm going to Beijing tomorrow.}}
<h3>And not with 是</h3>
<p>Because English says "I <i>am</i> eating", learners reach for <b>是</b>. But <b>是</b> only joins two nouns ("I am a student"). An action needs <b>在</b>, never <b>是</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Chinese</th></tr><tr><td>I'm a teacher.</td><td class="k">我是老师。</td></tr><tr><td>I'm working.</td><td class="k">我在工作。</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> copying "am + -ing" with 是. <b>我是吃饭</b> → <b>我在吃饭</b>.</div>
`,
